@cc [owner:spolu,label:security] workspace-isolation
Every filesystem operation MUST derive its workspace and grants from its authenticated session.
Neither matching grants nor knowledge of an object ID MUST permit cross-workspace access.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:security] live-authorization
Every server read MUST authorize and return data from one consistent SlateDB snapshot. Every server mutation MUST
validate current authorization through publication. Effective grants are explicit grants on the
object and its ancestors. Workspace keys alone authorize grant administration and session creation;
the server key alone authorizes workspace creation. Sessions MUST reject more than 512 distinct grants.
Expired, closed, and previous-process sessions MUST fail authorization.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:security] private-projections
Server-rendered root and /shared MUST expose only authorized entries; cached listings follow
kernel-owned-cache. Shared entries MUST use basename--object-id.
Synthetic entries MUST NOT be mutated. Inode parents MUST follow the visible projection, never expose
hidden canonical ancestry. Missing and inaccessible objects MUST produce indistinguishable errors.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:backend] atomic-filesystem-mutations
Every filesystem mutation MUST atomically publish all affected blocks, object records, child entries,
and explicit grant indexes in one SlateDB batch. Reads MUST NOT observe a partial mutation. Shrink followed by
extension MUST return zeros for removed bytes. Renames MUST preserve object identity and reject cycles.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:concurrency] compare-and-publish
Mutations of existing filesystem objects MUST compare expected versions under synchronization covering
authorization and publication. A mismatch MUST publish nothing. Each changed, surviving object MUST
advance its version once; new objects start at 1. Unrelated files MUST have independent versions. No automatic retry may replace a rejected expected version.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:performance;backend] memory-acknowledgement
Successful filesystem/workspace mutation RPCs MUST await SlateDB memory publication but MUST NOT
await remote durability. Buffered FUSE writes MAY acknowledge client RAM. Fsync MUST wait for preceding
kernel writeback and client write RPCs and propagate deferred errors without
flushing SlateDB. SlateDB's internal WAL
MUST remain enabled. Recovery MUST retain complete batches only; losing recent acknowledgements is
allowed. An application WAL, overlay, or persistence worker MUST NOT be introduced.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:backend;performance] bounded-slate-storage
All file contents MUST use SlateDB keys with at most 65536 bytes per block. No separate GCS blobs.
Requests, batches, caches, and retained handles MUST be bounded; large files MUST NOT require buffers
proportional to file size. Server cache budgets MUST be shared across workspaces and configurable.
Every restart MUST discard local caches and recover only from remote durable state.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:security;error-handling] transport-boundary
Credentials MUST appear only in RPC metadata or protected credential files, never URLs or logs.
Errors MUST NOT expose hidden names, grants, or backend details. Server version conflicts MUST remain
distinguishable from other RPC errors. Kernel writeback failures MUST reach fsync/close even if the
original write succeeded; the kernel may translate their errno. Ambiguous writes MUST return an error
and MUST NOT be automatically replayed.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:performance;security] kernel-owned-cache
FUSE MUST prioritize kernel metadata, directory, and content caching without a duplicate userspace
content cache. Cached data MAY remain accessible after remote changes or revocation, without a
freshness deadline. Every RPC MUST still use current server authorization. Observed session loss MUST
stop the mount, and reconnecting MUST NOT reuse cached state. Local mutations MUST maintain cache
coherence across aliases; no workspace-wide revision or periodic authorization polling.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:concurrency;error-handling] failed-writeback-stays-failed
Cached file data and queued writes MUST retain their base object version, advancing only for this
client's successful mutations. A failure MUST NOT refresh the version of pending dirty data. Retain
the first writeback error, conflict, or ambiguous file mutation across handles and reject subsequent
mutations while that state lives. Known metadata precondition errors need not poison cached state.
Recovery requires discarding the failed kernel state, by inode reclamation or remount. Regular-file
aliases MUST share one kernel inode. Unlink MUST remove the server object immediately; cached state
MUST NOT recreate it, and deferred writes MUST report the server's rejection.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:rust] fallible-rust
Rust code MUST propagate or explicitly handle fallible operations; unwrap() and expect() are forbidden.

@cc [owner:spolu,label:security;backend] gcs-adc-identity
GCS authentication MUST use GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS, local application-default credentials,
or the attached service identity. Unrelated SERVICE_ACCOUNT configuration MUST NOT override ADC.
